look at that it's empty  how am i going to get to work it's  stupidity  that we're all queuing up  but this is my job  is there petrol shortages no  it's mad isn't it  supply chain chaos soaring energy prices  and nhs buckling under a backlog the  threat of a covid19 resurgence  any one of these challenges would be  enough to cause serious concern but  they're all happening at once is it  going to last for months and could it  affect christmas i don't i think  everybody i think the supply chains are  very secure  but this is about far more than just  christmas it's about the risk of a much  wider winter crisis so what's going  wrong who could be impacted and how does  the government plan to avoid the worst  inflation has already been pushing up  living costs but as we head into the  winter those increases are likely to  continue  energy bills will rise significantly for  most people due to a huge spike in the  wholesale gas price and the cost of  everyday purchases from fuel to food to  furniture is being driven up by problems  caused in part by a shortage of drivers  post-brexit trade changes and global  supply chain pressures resulting from  the pandemic  the knock-on effect of all that is  uncertainty as the autumn rush for fuel  has demonstrated i've got  15 miles of petrol left i'm a community  physio  i need petrol to do my job i was on the  phone with my friend and he was driving  past the petrol station he was saying  about how kind of busy it was i know  what people are like because they tend  to panic don't they and then that causes  the shortage makes it all worse  while price increases and good shortages  will be felt by everyone those on low  incomes are going to be hit particularly  hard  the government has been heavily  criticized for pushing ahead with plans  to remove the 20 pounds weekly increase  to universal credit just at the time  these living cost pressures are kicking  in  the furlough scheme is also about to  come to an end  the government is hoping that employers  who have kept staff on furlough for this  long have done so because they intend to  take those staff back but if that  doesn't happen there could be hundreds  of thousands of people finding  themselves in a very difficult position  we're about to enter winter winter is  coming and people really should take  this seriously the government has been  clear that another winter lockdown would  only be considered as an absolute last  resort any responsible government  must prepare for all eventualities  given the rising living costs we've just  discussed they know that shutting down  the economy again would be catastrophic  particularly for those in the retail and  hospitality sectors who after last year  cannot afford to miss another crucial  christmas business period but there's  also an acceptance in westminster that  with the colder months people will mix  more indoors and that arise in cases is  inevitable the important thing to  remember is as we embark on the winter  months the virus haven't in inbuilt  advantage the big bet is that the  vaccine rollouts will mean the number of  people needing hospital treatment for  covid won't overwhelm the nhs but  there's no doubt that's a gamble  adding to that uncertainty is the  massive backlog that the nhs is  currently dealing with following the  pandemic and that could be compounded by  the seasonal pressures that normally  affect the health service an additional  five and a half billion pounds has been  given to the nhs in an effort to get to  grips with those ever-growing waiting  lists but ministers acknowledge things  could get worse before they get better  if there were serious disruption to  christmas as a result of a covid  resurgence or real chaos in the supply  chains there's no doubt that it would be  politically damaging for boris johnson  but even if that is avoided he'll be  aware people are likely to start feeling  the impact of these winter challenges in  their daily lives long before december  the 25th the government has weathered a  number of crises in the last two years  but that was largely thanks to an  acceptance amongst large swathes of the  electorate that the challenges posed by  the covent outbreak were unprecedented  the real political risk this winter is  that voters have lost patience and are  no longer willing to give ministers the  benefit of the doubt  when it comes to avoiding another  lockdown face covering rules vaccine  certificates for large venues and work  from home guidance could all be  reintroduced and on the issue of supply  shortages boris johnson says he's  confident the government can take steps  to ease the pressures a deal has been  done with suppliers in an effort to fix  the co2 shortage after fears were raised  by the food and drinks industry that it  could lead to empty supermarket shelves  and beer taps running dry  some support is also likely for energy  companies taking on the customers of  providers that have gone bust  and when it comes to wider supply chain  problems ministers have said they  believe market forces will ultimately be  the solution as businesses adapt  but even if that's right those changes  won't happen overnight so price rises in  the short term look unavoidable put all  of this together and you can see why  navigating the winter of 2021 could be  extremely challenging for the government  and for the millions of people being  impacted by a series of crises all  happening at  toilet  once  at uhd

